  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_section_1",
      "section_title": "Nasopharyngeal airway",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The nasopharyngeal airway (NPA) is a soft, hollow tube inserted through a nostril into the nasopharynx to help maintain upper-airway patency."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly called a nasal airway or nasal trumpet."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The NPA is an important OSCE instrument because it is often compared with the oropharyngeal airway (OPA/Guedel airway)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: An NPA can help maintain an airway in selected patients who cannot tolerate an OPA, including some patients with a gag reflex. However, it does not protect against aspiration or replace definitive airway management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Identification and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Name: Nasopharyngeal airway."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Route: Through the nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main function: To help maintain upper-airway patency."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Typical material: Soft, flexible medical-grade plastic or rubber."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Design: A hollow tube with a bevelled distal tip and a flared proximal end or flange."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Parts and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Proximal flange"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rests against the nostril and helps prevent the airway from advancing too far."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Hollow tube"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides a passage for airflow through the nasal route into the pharynx."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Bevelled distal tip"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Helps the tube pass through the nasal passage when correctly sized, lubricated, and inserted gently."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Types and sizes",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "NPAs are available in different lengths and internal diameters. Size selection is based on the patient's anatomy and the manufacturer's guidance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adult sizes: Used in appropriately sized adult patients."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pediatric sizes: Smaller devices selected for children according to their anatomy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Different materials: Flexibility and construction vary between products."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The size is commonly labelled in millimetres, often referring to the internal diameter. Colour coding, where present, varies by manufacturer."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An NPA may be considered in the following situations:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduced consciousness with an intact gag reflex: When an OPA is poorly tolerated but an airway adjunct is needed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Partial upper-airway obstruction: Particularly when the tongue and pharyngeal soft tissues contribute to obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assisted ventilation: To help maintain airway patency during bag-valve-mask ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Trismus or limited mouth opening: When oral access is difficult, provided nasal insertion is safe."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected patients requiring suctioning: When it is clinically appropriate and performed by a trained clinician."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An NPA is an adjunct, not a substitute for assessing and treating the cause of airway obstruction."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often better tolerated than an OPA in patients with a gag reflex."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Useful when the patient cannot open the mouth adequately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can help maintain airway patency during assisted ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flexible construction may facilitate insertion through a suitable nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Available in sizes for different patient anatomies."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages and limitations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause nasal bleeding or mucosal injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be difficult to insert through a narrow, obstructed, or traumatised nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect sizing or positioning may fail to relieve obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Does not reliably prevent aspiration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May be poorly tolerated in some conscious patients."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires clinical judgement and training, particularly in patients with facial trauma or bleeding risk."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Suspected basal skull fracture"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Traditionally treated as a major contraindication to blind NPA insertion because of the risk of intracranial placement in severe craniofacial injury. Use an alternative airway strategy and obtain expert assistance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Severe midface or nasal trauma"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid insertion through a potentially disrupted nasal passage; assess the injury and follow the relevant trauma airway protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Other precautions"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Significant nasal obstruction, recent nasal surgery, recurrent epistaxis, coagulopathy, or other substantial bleeding risks require careful assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE distinction: An intact gag reflex is a classic contraindication to routine OPA use, but not necessarily to NPA use. Suspected basal skull fracture is a key caution against blind NPA insertion."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "How to select the correct size",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Two considerations are important:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Length: A common external measurement is from the nostril to the tragus of the ear, following the local protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diameter: Choose a tube that fits the nostril without force. The tube's external diameter matters for passage through the nasal opening."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These measurements are estimates, not guarantees of correct internal positioning. Use the manufacturer's sizing guidance and clinical assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid an NPA that is too long or too wide. It may cause trauma, bleeding, or ineffective airway management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "How to insert an NPA — practical OSCE checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a clinical skill for trained personnel; practise on a manikin under supervision."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Insertion checklist",
                    "0/10"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Perform hand hygiene and use appropriate PPE."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the airway and determine whether an NPA is indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check for suspected basal skull fracture, severe midface trauma, nasal obstruction, and bleeding risks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select an appropriate size using anatomical landmarks and manufacturer guidance."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the nostrils and choose the more patent side."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lubricate the airway with a suitable water-soluble lubricant."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert gently along the floor of the nasal passage, directing posteriorly rather than upwards."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stop if resistance, significant pain, or bleeding occurs; never force the device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advance only as appropriate until the flange rests at the nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess airway patency, ventilation, oxygenation, and any complications."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_11",
              "group_title": "Step-by-step technique",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/nasopharyngeal_airway_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the procedure if the patient is sufficiently conscious, and prepare PPE."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess airway patency and screen for contraindications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Choose the correct size and inspect both nostrils."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lubricate the NPA with a suitable water-soluble lubricant."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert gently into the more patent nostril, directing the tube backwards along the floor of the nose, not upwards toward the bridge."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advance only while passage is easy and atraumatic."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stop immediately if there is significant resistance or bleeding. Do not force it; reassess and seek an alternative approach."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the flange rests at the nostril and reassess ventilation and airway patency."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Never force an NPA past resistance. Repeated attempts can cause significant nasal trauma and bleeding."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Cause or significance"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Epistaxis",
                      "Trauma to the nasal mucosa during insertion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nasal mucosal injury",
                      "Forceful insertion or incorrect sizing"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Turbinate injury",
                      "Advancing against the nasal structures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Airway obstruction",
                      "Incorrect size, displacement, or retained secretions"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gagging or vomiting",
                      "Pharyngeal stimulation in some patients"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "The NPA does not protect against gastric contents entering the airway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Poor ventilation",
                      "Malposition, persistent obstruction, or an inadequate seal during assisted ventilation"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Pediatric considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use a size appropriate to the child's anatomy, not age alone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Children's nasal passages are smaller and more vulnerable to trauma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use adequate lubrication and gentle insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not force the device through a narrow nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess for nasal trauma, obstruction, and bleeding risk before insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In neonates and young infants, use specialist guidance and age-appropriate airway equipment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "NPA versus OPA — high-yield comparison",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "NPA",
                    "OPA"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Full name",
                      "Nasopharyngeal airway",
                      "Oropharyngeal airway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Route",
                      "Nose",
                      "Mouth"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical design",
                      "Soft, hollow tube",
                      "Curved, usually rigid airway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gag reflex",
                      "May be used in selected patients with an intact reflex",
                      "Generally requires an absent gag reflex"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Trismus",
                      "May be useful if nasal passage is safe",
                      "May be difficult to insert"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Major concern",
                      "Nasal bleeding and facial/skull-base trauma",
                      "Gagging, vomiting, and oral trauma"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration protection",
                      "No",
                      "No"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Memory tip: OPA = oral route; NPA = nasal route. Both help maintain airway patency, but neither is a definitive airway."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Mnemonic: NOSE",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "N"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Nasal route — insert through the nostril."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "O"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Observe contraindications — especially suspected basal skull fracture and severe midface trauma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "S"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Size and lubricate — choose carefully before insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "E"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ease of passage — direct backwards along the nasal floor; never force."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE examiner questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is the function of an NPA?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To help maintain upper-airway patency by providing a passage through the nose into the pharynx."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Why might you choose an NPA instead of an OPA?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An NPA may be better tolerated in a selected patient with an intact gag reflex or limited mouth opening, provided nasal insertion is safe."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the major contraindication tested in examinations?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspected basal skull fracture is a key contraindication to blind NPA insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. In which direction should an NPA be inserted?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Posteriorly along the floor of the nasal passage, not upwards toward the bridge of the nose."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What should you do if resistance is encountered?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stop, withdraw as appropriate, reassess the nostril and size, and seek an alternative approach. Never force the device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Does an NPA protect against aspiration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It maintains airway patency but does not provide reliable protection against aspiration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Can an NPA be used in a patient with a gag reflex?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may be tolerated by selected patients with an intact gag reflex, unlike an OPA, but the patient's condition and the risks of nasal insertion must be assessed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_subsection_16",
          "subsection_title": "Mini OSCE scenario",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "nasopharyngeal_airway_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scenario: A drowsy patient has noisy breathing and limited mouth opening. An airway adjunct is being considered. You must identify an appropriate option and describe its insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Expected approach:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess airway, breathing, and the need for immediate resuscitation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Consider an NPA if clinically appropriate and nasal insertion is safe."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check for suspected basal skull fracture, significant midface trauma, and nasal bleeding risks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select the correct size and lubricate the device."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Insert gently along the floor of the nostril without forcing it."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess airway patency and ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Escalate if the airway remains obstructed or ventilation is inadequate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examiner's key point: Do not select an NPA automatically just because the mouth cannot be opened. First assess whether nasal insertion is safe."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}